<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">18 July in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 18 July, from 64 to 1936.</p></header>
<div class="tih-pinned-card"><p class="tih-pinned-label">International day</p><p class="tih-pinned-title">Nelson Mandela International Day</p><p class="tih-pinned-desc">Proclaimed by the United Nations in 2009 on the birthday of Nelson Mandela, this day invites individuals to devote 67 minutes of service to their communities — one minute for each year Mandela spent in public life.</p></div>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">64</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Great Fire of Rome breaks out</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Fire broke out near the Circus Maximus and raged for days, gutting ten of Rome's fourteen districts. Rumour blamed Nero; Tacitus records that he blamed the Christians, beginning their first persecution.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1290</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward I orders the expulsion of the Jews from England</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Edward I issued the Edict of Expulsion, ordering all Jews to leave England by 1 November. Several thousand were forced out, and Jews were not readmitted until the Cromwellian era of the 1650s.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1863</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The 54th Massachusetts leads the assault on Fort Wagner</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The 54th Massachusetts, one of the first Black regiments in the Union army, led a night attack on Fort Wagner near Charleston. The assault failed with heavy losses, but the regiment's courage changed Northern opinion.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1870</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The First Vatican Council defines papal infallibility</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The council adopted Pastor aeternus, declaring the Pope free from error when he defines doctrine on faith or morals ex cathedra. The decree strengthened papal authority and caused a schism with the Old Catholics.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1925</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The first volume of Hitler's Mein Kampf is published</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Hitler's autobiography and manifesto, written partly in prison, set out his antisemitism and his aim of conquering 'living space' in the east. Its ideas foreshadowed the policies of the Third Reich.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1936</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Military uprising launches the Spanish Civil War</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Following the army revolt in Morocco the day before, garrisons across Spain rose against the Republic. The war lasted nearly three years and ended in General Franco's dictatorship.</p></li></ol>
